# Documentation After Plans

After any Plan is executed, documentation must be read and updated to reflect
the changes. 

## Rules

- **Final task always:** When creating or executing a Plan, the final task
  must be: read and update relevant documentation.
- **Before closing a Plan:** read the docs that cover the changed area
  (`docs/`, README, design docs, API comments) and update them so they match
  current behavior, APIs, or design.
- **What to update:** fix outdated steps, names, examples, and TODOs; add
  or revise sections for new behavior; keep cross-references and checklist
  items accurate. Update `docs/CHANGELOG.md` **only** for user-visible,
  consensus, API, or security-relevant deltas (see
  [`95-documentation-lifecycle.mdc`](95-documentation-lifecycle.mdc)).
  Design-round archaeology stays in the plan doc. Completing a plan
  includes archive-or-contract: living SoTs stay and flip their Status
  banner; finished plans/preflights `git mv` to `docs/completed/` in the
  same change per `IMPLEMENTATION_INDEX.md` §8.
- **Scope:** prefer docs under `docs/` and the main README; update
  file-level or inline docs (Doxygen, module READMEs, Rust crate-level
  `//!` comments) when the change touches that code.
- **Stale-doc detection:** if a doc references behavior that no longer
  exists (e.g., a removed RPC field, a deleted RCT type), the doc update is
  not optional — the doc is wrong and will mislead readers.
- **Sweep the indexes, not just the text.** A correction is not landed until
  every surface that *points at* the corrected text agrees with it: the item's
  own **heading**, tables of contents, `docs/design/IMPLEMENTATION_INDEX.md`
  rows (rule 94), `docs/FOLLOWUPS.md` entry titles, cross-references from other
  docs, and the doc-comment headings above a corrected Rust item.

  **A corrected body under a stale heading is worse than an uncorrected
  item.** Scanning is how long documents are actually used — `FOLLOWUPS.md` is
  read by heading, and the body is read only if the heading says it is
  relevant. So a stale heading routes the correction to exactly the readers who
  did not need it, and hides it from the ones who did, while the file looks
  fixed to anyone grepping the body. Landed 2026-08-16: a FOLLOWUPS entry whose
  blocker had been removed in the body still read *"provisional pin awaiting
  the W₂ rig"* in its title.

- **A constant's doc is part of the edit, not adjacent to it.** When a constant's
  *value* changes, its own doc comment changes in the same edit — not in a later
  sweep. This is a narrower and more dangerous class than a stale comment
  elsewhere in the tree, because **proximity implies authority**: a reader trusts
  a comment attached to the thing it describes far more than one three files
  away, so the two disagreeing *inside one declaration* is the worst possible
  place for the disagreement to sit, and nothing checks the pair. Landed
  2026-08-19: `WITNESS_PREFIX_LEN` moved `40 → 8` while its own doc still read
  *"`r(32) ‖ count_le(8)`"* — a reader trusting the comment over the value would
  conclude the wire still carried a field that had been deleted.

- **The sweep covers claims about the present, never the record of what was.**
  A released `CHANGELOG` entry, a shipped release note, a dated audit record, or
  a "superseded text, retained" block describing a value **as it shipped** is
  *correct* — it is history, not staleness. A mechanical sweep that "fixes" every
  match corrupts the record **while reporting success**, which is worse than the
  staleness it set out to remove: the file now lies about what shipped, and
  nothing flags it. Before editing a match, ask whether the text asserts
  something *is* true or records something *was* true. Landed 2026-08-19: a
  transport-cap sweep matched a released `CHANGELOG` entry stating the old cap;
  the four live comments were corrected and that entry deliberately was not.

**Why this discipline exists at all, stated plainly:** the tree's **executable**
surface is checked continuously — by builds, tests, const-asserts, CI gates. Its
**descriptive** surface is checked *never*. One round in August 2026 turned up
**eight** stale numerals, and not one of them sat where any gate reads. A wrong
constant fails a build; a wrong comment *about* a constant becomes the next
person's starting assumption. Sweeping is the only thing standing in for a gate
there, which is why it keeps paying.

## Example

When a Plan has N tasks, the (N+1)th task is:

> "Read and update documentation (docs/*.md, README, design/API docs) to
> reflect the changes. Archive-or-contract per rule 95. Update
> CHANGELOG.md only if the change is user-visible, consensus, API, or
> security-relevant."

Complete that task before considering the Plan done.
